26. [x] kepub for the Kobo — implemented
    A sideloaded EPUB on a Kobo loses the store reading experience: no reliable
    progress, no stats. Converting to .kepub.epub on transfer is what Calibre
    users do for this. `prepare.rs` already unpacks, rewrites and repacks EPUB
    containers, so the machinery is adjacent. Narrow, but it is the difference
    between the book being on the Kobo and the book working on it.
    Implemented in `src/kepub.rs`, off unless `kepub` is saved. The rewrite is
    deliberately shallow: it walks the markup rather than parsing it, splicing
    spans around text between tags, so entities arrive exactly as they left and
    no document is re-serialized. Its safety is that every document it touches
    must still parse afterwards, or the whole conversion is refused.
    Three things only the real library showed. Documents are chosen from the
    manifest's media-type rather than from file names, because a book here names
    its chapters `.htm` and every one of them would have been skipped, leaving a
    book that looked converted while none of it was. roxmltree refuses a DOCTYPE
    unless asked, and nearly every EPUB 2 book carries one, so parsing needed
    allow_dtd or most of a real library would have been turned away. And a book
    that has been through Calibre's KoboTouchExtended is already divided; going
    over it again nested a second set of spans inside the first and left the
    Kobo counting the same words twice, so an already-divided document is now
    left alone.
    Verified across the ten EPUBs on the Kobo here: all convert, and 105
    documents come back byte-for-byte identical once the inserted spans are
    removed again, which is the invariant that makes a shallow rewrite safe.
    Not verified: how the device itself reads them. That needs ejecting and
    reading on the Kobo, which no test here can do.

27. [~] PDF to EPUB — done but for pictures
    Many PDFs have no reason to be PDFs. Many others cannot survive the trip,
    and a bad conversion is a book you do not find out is unreadable until you
    are away from your computer. So the feature is the triage, not the
    converter: classify before offering. Convertible is born-digital, one text
    column, an embedded outline, consistent margins. Poor is multi-column,
    footnote-heavy or full of tables — offer with a warning. Refuse page images
    with no text layer; OCR is a different project. Every signal is cheap from
    the page objects: text layer present, x-position clustering of text blocks,
    strings repeating at a fixed y (running headers to strip), an outline to
    become chapters.
    MIT licensing rules out MuPDF (AGPL) and Poppler (GPL). Pure Rust (lopdf,
    pdf) or pdfium (BSD-3, but a Chromium-scale C++ build). The output half
    already exists: zip, roxmltree and image are dependencies and `prepare`
    already writes EPUB containers. What is missing is PDF parsing plus reflow —
    paragraph joining from line gaps, dehyphenation, headings from font size.
    Convert alongside the PDF, never over it; keep both, show both, and let the
    result be read before it is sent anywhere. Depends on item 21.
    Classifier spiked in `src/pdf.rs` on lopdf 0.45 (MIT, pure Rust, no C++
    toolchain; its tree adds nom, indexmap, weezl and the like). It reads page
    structure only — where text sits, never what it says — and reports pages,
    pages carrying text, image-only pages, pages whose text starts in two
    separated bands, and whether an outline exists. A page is two-column when a
    left margin and a second margin past the middle both carry text and the
    gutter between them is quiet.
    Two things had to be right before the numbers meant anything, and neither
    was obvious. MediaBox is usually an indirect reference, so a page's width
    has to be dereferenced or every band is measured against the wrong scale.
    More importantly, Td and TD move in text space: their offsets are in units
    of the text matrix's own scale, so a jump of 20 at a scale of 11 is 220
    points. Added raw, the jump to the next column collapses to 20 and every
    page looks like one column. Both are covered by tests.
    Measured on real files: a 40-page two-column collection reported 35 of 38
    text pages as two-column once the scaling was right, against 13 before, and
    an 8-page single-column report came back convertible with an outline. Both
    verdicts match what pdftotext -layout shows.
    Conversion implemented on top of it: `pdf::convert` returns EPUB bytes and
    the verdict that went with them. `text.rs` is one walk of the content stream
    serving both judging and converting, so the two can never disagree about a
    page; `layout.rs` rebuilds prose from placed text; `write.rs` writes an
    EPUB 3 with an anchor per heading and a stable identifier, so converting the
    same PDF twice gives the same book. Text is decoded through the font's own
    encoding and ToUnicode CMap, bounded against decompression bombs.
    Three more things had to be right, each found by reading real output rather
    than by reasoning. Advance widths must come from the font: simple fonts
    carry `Widths` from `FirstChar`, composite fonts carry `W` on their
    descendant, and a composite font codes two bytes per glyph, so a byte count
    puts every run at twice its width. A space drawn as its own run must be kept
    rather than discarded as whitespace, because the words either side are
    otherwise left to be separated by the gap the space was holding open. And
    the short-line rule only means something in justified text: in ragged text
    every line ends somewhere different, so it is applied only when most lines
    end near the margin.
    Columns are read one at a time and the second is slid onto the first's
    margin, so indent and line-ending tests see one column. Reading a
    two-column page straight across weaves the columns together, which is the
    single worst thing a conversion can do; that is now a test. Because columns
    are handled, being in columns is no longer a concern on its own — only
    changing between columned and not, page to page, where the split is least
    certain.
    Measured on real files: a 40-page two-column collection converts to prose
    that reads correctly, and an 8-page report converts cleanly with its
    outline. Both pass Crossload's own EPUB validation and appear as ordinary
    books in the catalog, titled from the PDF's own metadata.
    Hyphens and chapters since settled. A hyphen left by a line break is
    decided by what the document does elsewhere: it is its own dictionary, and
    a compound it hyphenates where it had room will appear that way, while a
    word it broke appears whole. real-time survives and inconsis-tent closes up,
    both checked against the real files. A heading now starts a file, so a
    reader loads one chapter rather than the book; headings arriving in a run
    stay together, and prose before the first heading opens the book. The eight
    page report comes out as twelve named chapters.
    Pictures are not carried over, and the verdict now says so rather than
    dropping them quietly: a page of text that also shows an image makes the
    PDF convert poorly, so the dialog asks first. Tables are out of scope —
    rebuilding one needs cell geometry, and their text survives as loose lines.
    Extraction itself is deliberately not written. Neither PDF here holds a
    single image XObject, so it could only be developed against fixtures of its
    own making, and every bug in this work so far — text-space offsets,
    composite widths, spaces drawn as their own runs — was found by reading real
    output. Write it when there is a real PDF with figures to write it against,
    and expect DCTDecode to pass through as JPEG while Flate needs rebuilding
    through the image crate, with indexed and CMYK colour, SMask transparency
    and CCITT all waiting to be got wrong.
    Two bugs fell out of testing the figure warning. Page resources written
    inline rather than referenced were invisible to the image check, which is
    most pages that carry one. And a one-page document had its only line counted
    as both the page's head and its foot, reaching the repeat threshold and
    being discarded as a running head, so the page converted to nothing;
    furniture is repetition across pages, and one page can have none.
    Wired in. Every PDF is judged during discovery, on bytes already in hand,
    and the verdict is stored on the copy and in the cache (version 5), so the
    dialog never opens a file to draw itself. Xteink offers `convert to EPUB,
    copy` for a good one, asks for a y over a yellow prompt for a poor one
    naming what is wrong, and refuses an impossible one in red; `sync` applies
    the same rules without the question. `books::transfer` converts, publishes
    the EPUB into the import folder with the usual never-overwrite copy, and
    sends that, reporting both paths and that the PDF is untouched.
    Found while testing on the device: a copy the reader stores but will not
    list was counting as the book having arrived, so the two stranded PDFs made
    their books look present on Xteink and no conversion was ever offered.
    `Book::readable_at` now separates being somewhere from being readable there,
    and both the dialog and the sync planner use it.
    Verified against the real X4: a PDF converted, kept locally, sent over
    Wi-Fi, and listed by the device — /api/files reports isEpub true for it.
    Then simplified: discovery lists only EPUB on the reader, mirroring what the
    reader lists itself, so a PDF left there is skipped rather than shown as a
    copy. That removes the distinction between being at a place and being
    readable there, since nothing unlistable is discovered any more, and it
    spares the scan from downloading such a file over Wi-Fi only to hash
    something no book will ever match.

28. [-] Google Books — dropped
    Nothing on offer was actually sync. Uploading your own library to Play
    Books has no public API, only the web UI, so the round trip can never close:
    what remained was volume search for metadata and a read-only list of
    shelved titles. Neither justifies an OAuth flow, a client secret inside the
    binary, and a network call about books that are sitting on your own disk.
    Item 22 covers metadata from the files themselves, which is where it should
    come from.
    Worth remembering: where a publisher allows it, Play Books offers a
    purchased title as an ACSM download, and Crossload already fulfills ACSM
    with its own ADEPT implementation. Dropping that file in the import folder
    works today and needs no integration at all.

29. [x] Call the reader's place CrossPoint — implemented
    `Place::Xteink` named a location after one manufacturer while the protocol
    module beside it was `crosspoint.rs`, and CrossPoint runs on more than the
    X4: an M5Paper is the same reader as far as this tool is concerned.
    Renamed to `Place::CrossPoint`, which also reads better than Reader, since a
    Kobo is a reader too. The L K X column is now L K C. `--to xteink` still
    works as an alias, as the `xteink` command does.
    Three names are deliberately frozen, and say so where they are defined. The
    `META-INF/xteink-device-profile.txt` marker is carried by every device copy
    already on a reader or a card; renaming it would make those copies read as
    originals, losing the ◐ and their grouping with the books they came from.
    The `xteink-x4-images-v1:480x800:gray:jpeg85` profile names the X4's screen,
    which is what the conversion targets whatever the device is called. The
    private ADEPT state directory keeps its name so nobody's activation is
    orphaned.
    Two consequences. `crossload books --json` now reports `CrossPoint` where it
    reported `Xteink`, which is a break for anything scripted against it. And
    cache keys carry the place's label, so the reader's entries all missed once
    and were read again — a single slow scan, not a lasting cost.
    Found while doing it: the pseudo-terminal test saved `crosspoint.local` from
    the setup screen's default and then scanned whatever reader was on the
    network, which made it slow here and dependent on the room it ran in. It
    now checks the default is offered, then points the address at a port
    nothing answers on before saving.

30. [~] Optimize for the reader in hand — mechanism done, one screen known
    `prepare::optimize_images` converts every book to one fixed profile,
    `xteink-x4-images-v1:480x800:gray:jpeg85`, which is the X4's screen. Now
    that the tool says it serves any CrossPoint reader, that is wrong for any
    other one: an M5Paper has different geometry, so books prepared here arrive
    scaled for a device the reader is not. The tool would be quietly degrading
    images to the wrong size and calling it optimization.
    The reader already answers this. `/api/status` returns a `device` field —
    "X4" on this one — and `crosspoint::Status` parses it, so the profile can be
    chosen from what the device says it is rather than from a setting nobody
    knows how to fill in. The connection test in the settings screen already
    prints it back. A mounted card has no status endpoint, so the card path
    needs the device remembered from the last reachable reader, or asked for.
    Three things have to move together, and the third is a correctness trap.
    The profile string gains a per-device value, keeping
    `xteink-x4-images-v1:...` exactly as it is so copies already on devices
    still match. `optimize_images` already compares the stored profile byte for
    byte and re-optimizes anything that differs, which is right, though it
    compounds loss when the source is itself a device copy — `preferred` avoids
    that by favouring originals. And `cache::variant_key` is
    (sha, optimize, organized) with no profile in it, so as soon as there is
    more than one profile, a book optimized for one device would be served from
    the cache as if it were optimized for another. That key must carry the
    profile before a second one exists.
    Unknowns to settle first rather than assume: the actual screen geometry and
    colour depth of each supported device, and whether CrossPoint reports a
    distinct `device` string for each. Take them from hardware or from
    CrossPoint, not from memory.
    The mechanism is in, with the X4 as the only screen in it. `src/profile.rs`
    holds a screen as data — device name, size, marker — and `optimize` is now
    the screen to prepare for rather than a yes or no, so the thing being asked
    for travels with the request. The X4's marker is unchanged, so every device
    copy already on a reader is still recognized as one.
    The trap is closed: both cache keys carry the screen, so a copy made for one
    device can never be served as one made for another. That is tested against a
    second, invented screen, since there is no real one to test against yet.
    A name the tool does not know is refused when it is saved rather than on
    every command that reads it afterwards, which is how it was found: saving
    M5Paper left every later command failing until the setting was removed.
    When a book goes over Wi-Fi the reader is asked what it is — the one moment
    its name is free, since send already calls status — and it says so when that
    is not the screen the book was prepared for. A card cannot be asked.
    Still open, and deliberately: no second screen. The M5Paper's geometry is
    not known here, and inventing it would resize every image to a size no
    device has. Adding one now means one constant, a name that matches what
    CrossPoint reports for it, and nothing else.

## macOS verification update

Resolved on 2026-09-14. The Check workflow runs ubuntu-latest and macos-latest
on every push, and has done so throughout: build, the full test suite and the
pseudo-terminal smoke test. The current head is green on both, and the user has
run this version on a Mac by hand. The native ADEPT, Tears TUI, catalog and
caching work is therefore covered on macOS as far as automation can cover it.

What a Mac still has not done: attach a Kobo, transfer to a reader, or run a
packaged archive. The tag workflow packages both macOS architectures but has
never been run.

One macOS failure is on record and was never reproduced: commit fe4d5bc (item
13) failed the pseudo-terminal test at quit, where Ctrl+C is sent and the
process must exit within ten seconds. Linux passed the same commit and the four
pushes since have passed on macOS, including that exact sequence. Quit while
work is still running is a pending quit that waits for its worker, so a slow
runner and a refresh in flight is a plausible cause; ten seconds is not a
generous timeout. If it recurs, raise the timeout and print the final screen
and status line rather than only the traceback.

